    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Headaches, worse in the morning, for about two years, with episodes over six months of tingling and weakness of the right hand and leg lasting a few minutes, one with twitching of the right fingers and one with difficulty finding words; mild right-hand weakness on examination; cause not yet established; urgent MRI and EEG booked</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R29.818</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting concern at the index visit, superseded by the MRI and EEG diagnosis; her documented headaches with transient right-sided paralysis from 1839 are echoed.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Meningioma of the left frontoparietal convexity over the hand area of the motor cortex, 3.1 x 2.7 x 2.6 cm, with moderate vasogenic edema; resected (Simpson grade I); meningothelial meningioma, CNS WHO grade 1; synthetic modern counterpart of the supposed tumour of the left hemisphere on her death certificate</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>D32.0</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis. Her symptoms and the certificate&apos;s wording are documented (D); a brain tumour is a retrospective interpretation (R) disputed by the hemiplegic migraine and stroke hypothesis, and no tissue exists, so this meningioma is not presented as her historical diagnosis.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Focal epilepsy due to the left convexity meningioma: focal aware sensory and motor seizures of the right hand and arm, one with speech arrest; EEG with left central sharp waves; on levetiracetam</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>G40.109</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis echoing her documented convulsive seizures and transient right-sided paralysis; the hemiplegic migraine interpretation is recorded as R.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Acute viral gastroenteritis for two days, with vomiting of one levetiracetam dose, mild dehydration, and no seizure; resolved in four days</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>A08.4</td><td>S — Synthetic common illness chosen to teach antiseizure medicine adherence during vomiting; it echoes no documented illness.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

